When a flex container’s items have different order values, Ladybird
sorts them into order-modified document order for every flex direction,
and reverses a reversed direction only when it places them along the
main axis. Blink does the same: Its FlexChildIterator yields children in
order-modified document order, and FlexLayoutAlgorithm::ApplyReversals()
reverses the items of each line afterward. WebKit’s FlexLayout::
buildFlexItemList() stable-sorts the flex items by order, and its
FlexFormattingContext::computeFlexItemRects() flips a reversed direction
when it turns flow-relative positions into physical ones. Gecko’s
nsFlexContainerFrame::GenerateFlexLines() walks the children with a
CSSOrderAwareFrameIterator, and its FlexboxAxisTracker reverses the main
axis in LogicalPointFromFlexRelativePoint().

Item positions match Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5
in 36 cases: row, row-reverse, column, column-reverse, row-reverse with
wrap, wrap-reverse, row-reverse with rtl, rtl, and row-reverse in
vertical-lr, each with mixed, negative, equal and two groups of order
values. Ladybird paints overlapping items in order-modified document
order, as Firefox and WebKit do; Chrome paints the items of a reversed
row in the opposite order.
